  "image_name": "193efbafd959647cd368befcee61ecc0.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how mercury moves into and within a tree, emphasizing that the atmosphere is the dominant source. The central tree graphic indicates about 89% of uptake comes from the air and only about 11% from roots. In leaves, gaseous Hg(0) enters mainly through stomata, while both Hg(0) and Hg(II) can deposit on the surface or cuticle. Some of the absorbed mercury appears to be oxidized, reduced, re-emitted, or bound in surface layers, with most retained mercury shown in internal tissues rather than the outer surface. The leaf cross section highlights epidermis, mesophyll, veins, and stomata as the main compartments involved.\n\nThe root and soil diagrams show Hg²⁺ in soil solution interacting with root exudates such as citrate and malate, and possibly crossing membranes via largely unknown transporters. A root cross section suggests mercury must pass through outer tissues, cortex, endodermis, and vascular tissues, with barriers like the Casparian strip influencing movement.\n\nThe trunk schematic shows mercury translocated upward in xylem and redistributed from foliage, while concentrations seem to decrease from bark inward. Overall, the image presents a whole-plant pathway for mercury uptake, transport, storage, and re-emission.",
